WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.540 --> 00:00:03.300
My name is Akhilan
Boopathy, and my major

00:00:03.300 --> 00:00:06.750
is in electrical engineering
and computer science.

00:00:06.750 --> 00:00:11.140
My project is on robustness
of neural networks.

00:00:11.140 --> 00:00:12.930
Neural networks have
become increasingly

00:00:12.930 --> 00:00:15.840
popular for a variety
of tasks, most notably

00:00:15.840 --> 00:00:18.120
for image classification.

00:00:18.120 --> 00:00:19.710
Unfortunately,
they're susceptible

00:00:19.710 --> 00:00:21.780
to adversarial attacks.

00:00:21.780 --> 00:00:23.910
This is when the input
to a neural network

00:00:23.910 --> 00:00:28.380
is slightly perturbed in order
to cause a misclassification.

00:00:28.380 --> 00:00:30.780
This has serious
implications in applications

00:00:30.780 --> 00:00:33.840
like self-driving cars, for
instance, where adversaries

00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:36.240
could cause accidents.

00:00:36.240 --> 00:00:40.020
Fortunately, we can find
regions in the input

00:00:40.020 --> 00:00:45.280
space that are certified to
be robust to perturbations.

00:00:45.280 --> 00:00:49.830
So for example, in this image,
this ostrich, if perturbed,

00:00:49.830 --> 00:00:52.290
will still be
classified as an ostrich

00:00:52.290 --> 00:00:55.370
within these green regions.

00:00:55.370 --> 00:00:57.320
This raises the
question, how do we

00:00:57.320 --> 00:00:59.630
efficiently train
neural networks to have

00:00:59.630 --> 00:01:03.470
these large regions of
certified robustness?

00:01:03.470 --> 00:01:06.080
Our approach is
to use two margins

00:01:06.080 --> 00:01:10.280
to quantify the uncertainties
in the network at each layer.

00:01:10.280 --> 00:01:13.640
One margin tracks a level
of linear uncertainty,

00:01:13.640 --> 00:01:17.960
while another margin tracks
a level of nonlinearity.

00:01:17.960 --> 00:01:19.970
These two margins
can then be used

00:01:19.970 --> 00:01:23.240
to approximate an upper bound
on the worst-case performance

00:01:23.240 --> 00:01:27.440
of the network or the
worst-case adversarial loss.

00:01:27.440 --> 00:01:30.200
Here, we show our
current results.

00:01:30.200 --> 00:01:33.410
On the y-axis, we have
certified accuracies,

00:01:33.410 --> 00:01:35.750
which are essentially
the fraction of points

00:01:35.750 --> 00:01:39.080
in a test set that
we can show or prove

00:01:39.080 --> 00:01:43.520
to be robust to adversaries
of a certain size.

00:01:43.520 --> 00:01:46.520
On the x-axis, we show the
assumed adversarial attack

00:01:46.520 --> 00:01:48.500
size.

00:01:48.500 --> 00:01:51.860
The red dotted lines show
the current state-of-the-art

00:01:51.860 --> 00:01:54.770
certified robust
training method,

00:01:54.770 --> 00:01:58.640
while the blue solid
line shows our method.

00:01:58.640 --> 00:02:01.700
As illustrated, for
large perturbation sizes,

00:02:01.700 --> 00:02:04.460
we can achieve higher
certified accuracy bounds

00:02:04.460 --> 00:02:07.370
compared to the current
state of the art.

00:02:07.370 --> 00:02:10.610
In addition, our method only
takes three times the training

00:02:10.610 --> 00:02:12.680
time of conventional
training, which

00:02:12.680 --> 00:02:14.540
is on par with the
current state of art

00:02:14.540 --> 00:02:17.120
in terms of training time.

00:02:17.120 --> 00:02:20.090
Next, we hope to scale this
method to larger networks

00:02:20.090 --> 00:02:22.430
and larger data sets.

